{
  "case_id": "2021-21-248",
  "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:26 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David H. Thompson",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "North Carolina law designates the state as necessary parties -- the state -- the Petitioners as agents of the state and as necessary parties in all actions challenging state statutes. When such actions are filed in state court, the Petitioners are defendants and necessary parties. This lawsuit, however, was filed in federal court, and when the Petitioners sought to intervene, they were denied and a strong presumption was applied against their intervention."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: Counsel, two things. One is what do you do with Wallace v. Bone, a North Carolina supreme court case that says the state legislature cannot represent the state? And I thought that that was the basis of the governor's claim that the law was unconstitutional -- that this representative law was unconstitutional."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOTOMAYOR: That -- that's not -- no, that's not what their point is. I think, if the Attorney General wasn't defending the law, there'd be another case. That's what the court below said. It would be a different case"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "David H. Thompson",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0000-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Federal Rule of Civil Procedure 24(b)(2) reflects the interest in administering a law. Those are the two interests we have here. They're not created by the state -- by state law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MR. THOMPSON",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "States can create interests. We can see that in the text of Rule 24 because it talks about property, it talks about transactions, which would include contracts. Both of those are the traditional province of state law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 0.97,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: The Rules of Federal Procedure -- note where it is. It's under (b), permissive intervention, not what we're talking about, which is intervention of right. All right. Focusing on that for a second, what is it you want this Court to hold?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "MS. THEODORE",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "From Rule 24's inception through today, a single principle has guided interpretation of the adequacy prong. When a proposed intervenor's interest is identical to one that's already represented in the case, we presume that the existing representative is adequate, and that common-sense presumption holds particular force when the existing representative is a state official charged ethically and legally with defending state interests."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, you said right at the outset that there's a federal interest that people on each side of the case speak with a single voice, right? Where did -- where did that come from?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Barrett",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BARRETT: But isn't their position that even if it wasn't the Attorney General defending the Board of Elections, that it would still -- they would still be entitled to intervention?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. THEODORE: I -- well, I think North Carolina law clearly says that the Attorney General is the authorized representative of the state Board of Elections. And North Carolina law says that the Attorney General represents the state in any case in which the state's a party or interested or its agencies are a party. So --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We do not have an issue with them asserting a legislative interest insofar as the question is does that pose a constitutional problem. We agree that they are entitled to assert a legislative interest."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And would you also agree that under the intervention rule, that would be perfectly permissible?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0003-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 71,
        "start": 71
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the district court denied that motion as moot and stayed the case until this case is resolved by this Court."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "79fc0b1b8362518878448abb22147526f16b00a8666702c08abdeb6016459daa",
      "evidence_id": "ev-79fc0b1b8362-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 75,
        "start": 75
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "our position is that, as they have brought this case to the Court asking only for mandatory intervention on behalf of the state, which we think gives rise to a significant constitutional problem, the only proper outcome for this Court is to deny mandatory intervention."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2021-21-248",
